If brew services start neo4j worked but your Python code can’t connect to bolt://localhost:7687, the Neo4j instance might be:
	•	Running on a different Bolt port
	•	Not listening on the Bolt port at all
	•	Using authentication or a different password
	•	Failing silently due to a config issue

⸻

✅ Steps to Diagnose and Fix It

1. Check Which Port Neo4j Is Using

Run:

lsof -iTCP -sTCP:LISTEN -P | grep neo4j

Look for a line like:

java    12345 username   97u  IPv6 0x...  TCP *:7687 (LISTEN)

If it’s not :7687, update your Python uri:

uri = "bolt://localhost:<correct-port>"


⸻

2. Confirm Bolt Is Enabled in the Neo4j Config

Open the config file:

open /opt/homebrew/etc/neo4j/neo4j.conf

Ensure these lines are uncommented and correct:

dbms.connector.bolt.enabled=true
dbms.connector.bolt.listen_address=:7687

If you change anything, restart:

brew services restart neo4j


⸻

3. Confirm Password

Neo4j may ask for a new password on first run. If you haven’t set one or forgot it:
	•	Visit http://localhost:7474
	•	Login as neo4j and set a new password
	•	Then in your Python script:

Neo4j_Pass = "yourpassword"


⸻

4. Test Python Connection Separately

Test with this minimal code:

from neo4j import GraphDatabase

uri = "bolt://localhost:7687"
driver = GraphDatabase.driver(uri, auth=("neo4j", "yourpassword"))

with driver.session() as session:
    result = session.run("RETURN 1")
    print(result.single())


⸻

Let me know the output of the port check or if you want a script to auto-detect and connect to the correct port.
